Z||000000||Jay||04-08-2001||05:30 PM||||Since we've got a flurry of posts going on about Northstars and manual tranny's I figured I would ask: Anyone out there look at a turbo installation for the northstar?  DesignOne has a twin turbo kit, but they don't have any photos or info on their web site and are pretty hush, hush about it (Can't blame them).  How about Supercharging?  I've herd that the new Cadillac's will be supercharged.  If so, will the blower fit on an older engine or will it be an entirely different set up?  Your thoughts?||||reg||14
Z||000001||Will||04-09-2001||04:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jay:<BR><B>Since we've got a flurry of posts going on about Northstars and manual tranny's I figured I would ask: Anyone out there look at a turbo installation for the northstar?  DesignOne has a twin turbo kit, but they don't have any photos or info on their web site and are pretty hush, hush about it (Can't blame them).  How about Supercharging?  I've herd that the new Cadillac's will be supercharged.  If so, will the blower fit on an older engine or will it be an entirely different set up?  Your thoughts?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I've been musing about a turbo setup. IMO, anyone who wants to boost a '99 or older engine beyond about 6 psi is crazy.<BR>I think a much more receptive engine would be the Y2K and later Northstars. They have forged cranks and rods, and, most importantly, can make full power on 87 octane, giving 4-6 more points of octane to play with depending on the local availability of 93.<BR>If you wanted to go all the way, you could obtain an LMP crankshaft, LMP rods, LMP pistons, and LMP cams. Then you would have a shorter stroke/longer rod for even more knock resistance; probably lower compression, too.<P>There was a picture floating around the forum of Gary Zumalt with a Northstar that had a BIG supercharger on top.<BR>I have no idea what future Cadillacs are going to use.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Slammed Fiero||04-09-2001||05:00 PM||||If you want to really uncork the HP of the N* forget the Turbo set ups and what not , too many people having trouble with engines running at less than full power as it is. The motor is used quite a bit in racing. I have seen a few EX imsa cars floating around with basically stock long blocks pushing 600hp. Look at Tim allens "TAD" designed caddy , while it's straight line performance wasn't very impressive , by bumping compression of the engine , and a set of headers he was able to curn out 400hp. Apperently these motors can spin to 7500rpm with no problem in stock form. It doesn't make sense for GM to tune them in this manner , but the potential for massive gains in HP are there without bolting on turbo's or superchargers. Get the motor Running 100% then go from their. IF you want to find some hiddden HP I would look at the injection system. The articles I have on the IMSA cars show them using 1 TB per cylinder much like a set of webber carbs. I would start by talking to TWM induction about what they can do in the way of an injection setup. I forget what TIm allen runs compression , But I believe it was around the 12.5:1 mark. With modern engine management it shouldn't be a problem to do this on the street. <P>I think a lot of people want to walk before they can crawl.. wait til someone figures out the electronic issues before boling on power adders<P><BR>Jonathan M ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jay||04-09-2001||05:50 PM||||Slammed I've gotta agree.  I'm just tired of searching for people that will work on the electronics on the Northstar.  Those who got it won't share it and those who will share it don't fully got it.  DesignOne never got back to me about the computer they've got (which has electronics worked out).  I'm playing wiht the Idea of a turbo for the future.<BR>Will, 93 Octaine fuel in my area is no problem.  I've already been looking at the LMP parts...can you say expensive!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||04-10-2001||10:20 AM||||Obviously I wouldn't turbo the darn thing before I got it running right, now would I?<P>Remember, it's only 280 cubic inches. I have no doubt that it can spin to 8K and beyond and put out over 450 hp N/A, but would it be a street engine with the cams it would take to do that?<P>A turbocharged street engine is still a street engine, with all the good manners and good mileage of a street engine.<BR>Big cams, high redline and a lot of power are impressive, but they don't make a good street engine without variable cam/valve timing.<P>What I would <I>really</I> like to do is retrofit the variable intake cam timing apparatus from GM's straight 6 (coming soon to GMC's near you) to both cams of the N*, probably with a custom controller. That would be the best of both worlds.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ray b||04-10-2001||03:37 PM||||c shelby has the S-1 with arrora [4.0 N*] with a supercharger now.<BR>NOS may be best and cheapest way to go for extra power on a N*.||||reg||1
